help.open = Opens the help window.
help.title = dubdsr — Help
help.intro = Numbers 1 to 27 match the picture. The section slider (28) and the time (29) appear when needed. Hover a control in the main window to read the same text.
help.t.gpu = GPU
help.t.path = Path field
help.t.progress = Progress bar
help.t.status = Status line
help.t.slider = Section slider
help.t.time = Time
help.gpu = NVIDIA card name and its memory, in GB. Nothing to set. If CUDA is unavailable, stacking will fail.
help.target = Sun and Moon are two separate profiles. Each keeps its own path and every setting. Click the list to switch.
help.path = Path of the AVI video or the photo folder. Paste a path, then click elsewhere to open it.
help.browse = Opens a SeeStar AVI video, including files over 1 GB. The TIFF is written next to the video: name_stacked.tiff.
help.folder = Opens a folder of RAW photos: CR2, CR3, NEF, NRW, ORF, PEF, ARW, DNG, RAF, RW2. Subfolders are ignored. The TIFF is written inside the folder.
help.full = Stacks the whole video or every photo in the folder. The frame limit can still cut the start.
help.section = Limits stacking to a range, including both ends. The slider then replaces the hint.
help.keep = Share of the sharpest frames, from 10% to 100%, in steps of 5. A lower value keeps a sharper disc. First launch: 10%. At least 3 frames are kept when there are enough.
help.limit = Maximum number of frames read from the start of the zone. 0 removes the cap. Useful for a short test. From 0 to 100,000.
help.bayer = Color mosaic pattern. Auto is recommended: a video is analyzed, a RAW folder uses the pattern stored in the files. A wrong pattern leaves a green or magenta grid. With debayer off, the stack stays gray.
help.margin = Widens the square around the disc, from 1% to 30%. Used only when auto crop is on. First launch: 5%.
help.crop = Finds the disc and cuts a square around it. Off: the whole frame is stacked. On at first launch.
help.debayer = On: rebuilds Bayer color. Off: the stack and the TIFF stay grayscale. On at first launch.
help.wb = Pulls the disc toward neutral gray and removes the green cast. On for the Moon, off for the Sun. No effect on a gray stack.
help.drizzle = On: the output is three times wider and taller. Off: same size as the crop. On at first launch. The file is much larger.
help.flatten = Reduces the bright bulge at the center. 0% turns it off. First launch: 70% for the Sun, 0% for the Moon. Above 0%, Sun compression and highlight protection no longer apply.
help.gamma = Above 1, dark areas lift. 1 leaves the curve straight. From 0.50 to 3.00. First launch: 1.20.
help.sharpen = Strengthens detail at four sizes. 0 turns sharpening off. From 0 to 6. First launch: 2.00. A high value also boosts noise.
help.radius = Size of the finest detail. 1 stays fine, 3 and above targets broader structures. From 0.5 to 8. First launch: 1.0. No effect when sharpening is 0.
help.solar = Compresses the Sun's highlights. 0 turns it off. From 0 to 50. Applies only when center flattening is 0%.
help.black = Lowers the background further. From 0.0% to 20.0%. First launch: 0.1%. A high value eats the limb.
help.background = Estimates the sky level in the corners and subtracts it, then blacks out the area outside the disc. On at first launch.
help.protect = On: the brightest center is not clipped. Off: the white point is 99.7%. Applies only when center flattening is 0%.
help.stack = Starts stacking. The button stays inactive while the job runs. The 16-bit TIFF is written only at the end, and a new run of the same source replaces the file.
help.progress = Moves from 0% to 100%. It can stay on the same stage for a while during tone and TIFF writing.
help.status = At rest: Ready. During the job it names the stage. At the end: frames kept, Bayer pattern, and TIFF path.
help.preview = Before stacking, the middle image of the source. A video is shown in gray. A RAW shows its thumbnail when it has one. After success, the result scaled for the screen. The TIFF on disk stays 16-bit.
help.slider = Two handles mark the start and the end, both included. Drag the nearest handle. The preview follows it. A video shows the time, a folder the file name.
help.time = Elapsed time while the job runs, total time after success, or time already spent if the job stopped.
